{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Eleh</b> — he remembers the ancient days when he went up on pilgrimage." "<b>The meaning of besakh</b> — like the enclosure of the Sabbath that was on the Temple Mount. Some say: with the entirety of the celebrants, like סך חשבון (the sum of a reckoning). Ibn Labrat said: for אדדם is doubled and it is like אדום. But who would say ידום, for a pe-ayin verb is never doubled, and what is the meaning of saying ידום followed by 'with a voice of joy and praise'?! And what is near to me: that it is mishnaic language, [meaning] 'I move,' as if he said: אדדה with them to the house of Elohim, and like it: אדדה all my years, and the word comes from the nifal binyan, like וילחמוני חנם."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 42, :notes {1 "Ibn Labrat's argument about אדדם being 'doubled' like אדום is textually difficult; translated hyper-literally without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:30:36.338355780Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}